WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.161
Risk combines likelihood, impact, and context.

2
00:00:03.161 --> 00:00:06.785
A highly likely event with low impact may be manageable.

3
00:00:06.785 --> 00:00:12.567
A low-probability event with catastrophic impact may still deserve serious attention.

4
00:00:12.567 --> 00:00:15.112
Context decides how much risk matters.

5
00:00:15.112 --> 00:00:22.205
The same technical failure might be tolerable in a prototype and unacceptable in a production safety system.

6
00:00:22.205 --> 00:00:28.759
Expected value is a high-level way to think about outcomes by combining likelihood and consequence.

7
00:00:28.759 --> 00:00:34.695
If an option has a modest chance of a large benefit and a small downside, it may be attractive.

8
00:00:34.695 --> 00:00:41.249
If an option has a high chance of a small benefit and a meaningful chance of severe damage, it may not be.

9
00:00:41.249 --> 00:00:48.805
Expected value is not a magic formula, but it helps people avoid focusing only on the most emotionally vivid outcome.

10
00:00:48.805 --> 00:00:51.272
Average expectations are not enough.

11
00:00:51.272 --> 00:00:55.898
Tail risks are rare but severe outcomes that can dominate the decision.

12
00:00:55.898 --> 00:01:02.760
A plan may look fine on average but still expose the organization to a worst credible case it cannot absorb.

13
00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:11.858
Security, operations, safety, and continuity planning all require attention to what could happen at the edge, not just what is most likely.

14
00:01:11.858 --> 00:01:15.559
Fragile decisions fail sharply when conditions change.

15
00:01:15.559 --> 00:01:22.729
Resilient decisions preserve options, limit blast radius, include monitoring, and make recovery possible.

16
00:01:22.729 --> 00:01:25.505
Mitigation reduces likelihood or impact.

17
00:01:25.505 --> 00:01:29.745
Contingency planning prepares action if the risk materializes.

18
00:01:29.745 --> 00:01:36.684
Risk tolerance defines how much uncertainty and downside the decision maker is willing and able to accept.

19
00:01:36.684 --> 00:01:42.004
A responsible decision process considers upside, downside, and survivability.

20
00:01:42.004 --> 00:01:52.413
It asks what success looks like, what failure looks like, what signals would show the situation is changing, and what action would be taken if assumptions break.

21
00:01:52.413 --> 00:01:54.880
Good risk thinking is not fear-based.

22
00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:58.966
It is a practical way to keep action aligned with consequences.
